Vapor-phase linker exchange of the metal-organic framework ZIF-8: a solvent-free approach to post-synthetic modification
ZIFs are comprised of divalent metal cations coordinated by imidazolate linkers.[1,2] ZIF-8 is arguably the most well-studied member of the ZIF family. It consists of Zn(II) and 2-methyl imidazolate (mlm) connected in a sodalite (sod) topology. Examples of other Zn(II)-based sod ZIFs with different linkers are ZIF-7 (benzimidazolate), ZIF-71-sod (4,5-dichloro imidazolate), ZIF-65 (2-nitro imidazolate) and ZIF-90 (2-carboxaldehyde imidazolate).[3-7] While these materials are similar in unit cell and pore dimensions, their chemical properties differ due to the different imidazolate linkers. PSLE enables mixed-linker ZIFs and, in some cases, materials not accessible through direct synthesis.[8] This solution-based approach requires large solvent volumes and the linker incorporation preference can be hard to rationalize due to solvent effects. Interestingly, several ZIF materials can be synthesized through vapor-based and other solvent-free methodologies.[9-12] Here we present a novel, solvent-free approach to post-synthetic linker exchange in ZIF-8 through exposure to linker vapor.
Initial VPLE tests were performed by exposing ZIF-8 to 2-carboxaldehyde imidazole (CHO-Hlm) vapor (Figure 1). 2-Methyl imidazole (Hmlm) and CHO-Hlm have similar dimensions, small enough to allow diffusion through the ZIF pore network (Table S1). As CHO-Im is prone to side-reactions.[13-15] VPLE trials (24 h) were performed at different temperatures. Reaction at 353 and 373 K resulted in a yellow ZIF powder, similar to what is obtained
through direct synthesis or PSLE with CHO-Hlm in solution.[16,17] X-ray diffraction (XRD) confirms that the sod topology is maintained after VPLE. \(^1\mathsf{H}\) NMR analysis of the acid-digested samples revealed the incorporation of intact CHO-Im (Figure S6). At \(393\,\mathrm{~K~}\) the sample turned brown (Figure S7) and XRD and electron microscopy reveal signs of degradation together with a drop in BET surface area from 1306 to \(638\;\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}\) (Figures S8-10), similar to PSLE in MeOH.[16] For further VPLE experiments, 353 K was used.
VPLE was monitored as a function of time for ZIF-8 crystals ranging in size from \(<100\ \mathsf{n m}\) to \(>100\ \upmu\mathrm{m}\) . For each time point, the linker ratio was analyzed by \(^1\mathsf{H}\) -NMR (Figure S11). A clear dependence of the linker exchange kinetics on the particle size would indicate diffusion limitations during the exchange process. In contrast to this intuitive expectation, crystals of different sizes show similar exchange kinetics and end up with an identical fraction of CHO-Im in the framework \((84\pm6\;\%\) ; Figure 2). FTIR analysis (Figure S11) confirms the progressive decrease of mlm bands (e.g., at \(991~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) ), accompanied by increasing signals characteristic of CHO-Im incorporation (e.g., at \(1667~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) ). This size-independence is in contrast with the mass transport limitations reported for linker exchange in solution. [16] In that case, after \(24~\mathsf{h}\) of exchange, the CHO-Im linker fraction was \(70\,\%\) for nanocrystals compared to only \(26\,\%\) for micron-sized crystals.
VPLE consists of the following steps: (i) adsorption of the incoming linker, (ii) exchange of protons between adsorbed CHO-HIm and the mIm linkers in the ZIF-8 framework, and (iii) removal of protonated HImIm. The size-independence of the VPLE kinetics suggests that the phenomena related to mass transport in the ZIF-8 pores are not rate-determining, i.e., linker adsorption, diffusion, and desorption. Adsorption of the incoming linker is evidenced by XRD, through reversible changes in the low-angle diffraction peaks of ZIF-8 upon exposure to CHO-HIm vapor (Figure S13). When the framework mIm linker is protonated, the bond to the Zn(II) cations is weakened and the HImIm molecule is volatilized. Since the sod topology and particle size distribution are maintained before and after VPLE (Figure S14), the Zn(II) cations likely have no or limited mobility – as expected in the absence of a liquid phase. In other words, VPLE occurs in a single-crystal to single-crystal manner, since MOF dissolution-recrystallization cannot occur in the absence of a solvent.
After \(72\;\mathrm{h}\) of VPLE, the BET surface area decreases slightly, from \(1430\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) to \(1340\pm20\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), averaged over the different particle sizes. This slight decrease is expected based on the BET surface area of pure ZIF-90 \((1291\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\). The incorporation of CHO-Im leads to higher \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) uptake in the low-pressure range (<0.1 P/P0; Figure S15). Similarly, the MeOH uptake gradually transitions from the ZIF-8 to the ZIF-90 isotherm (Figure S16). VPLE works equally well for ZIF-8 films deposited on quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) substrates (Figures S17-18). The gravimetric MeOH isotherms recorded for the resulting ZIF layers follow a similar trend as the VPLE ZIF powders (Figure S19).
VPLE requires (i) that the incoming linker protonates the framework linker, (ii) that the protonated forms of both linkers are sufficiently volatile, and (iii) that the incoming linker can coordinate the Zn(II) cations while maintaining the sod topology. Since vapor pressure data is not readily available for other imidazole linkers of interest, their melting point was used instead as a rough estimator. Combining the pKa, melting point and topology criteria suggests a range of incoming linker candidates (Figure 3), of which 4,5-dichloro imidazole (4,5-diCl-im), benzimidazole (bzIm) and 2-nitro imidazole \((\mathsf{N O}_{2^{-}}\mathsf{I m})\) were selected for VPLE tests. The sod ZIFs formed by these linkers are referred to as ZIF-71-sod (100 % dCl-Im), ZIF-7 (100 % bzIm), and ZIF-65-Zn (100 % \(\mathsf{N O}_{2^{-}}\mathsf{I m}\)).[18–21] For each linker, VPLE was performed below the melting point and the thermal stability limit (Table S2). All linkers were successfully incorporated in ZIF-8 after \(72\;\mathrm{h}\), although to different extents: \(68\%\) for \(\mathsf{N O}_{2^{-}}\mathsf{I m}\), \(44\%\) for bzIm, and \(37\%\) for dCl-Im. More bulky linkers and linkers that can form ZIF topologies other than sod seem to be incorporated less. Nevertheless, only the sod topology was
