observed, thus supporting the notion of a single-crystal to single-crystal transformation (Figures S20-21). As a negative control, VPLE was attempted with 2-ethylimidazole (Helm). As expected based on the lower acidity of this linker, no exchange was observed
The absence of adsorbed or proton-shuttling solvent molecules results in a different structure energy landscape during VPLE compared to linker exchange in solution.[22,23] For instance, exposure of ZIF-8 to vapor of unsubstituted imidazole (Hlm) results in a transformation to the mixed-linker MOF ZIF-61 with a donor-znI topology.[24] Although the same exchange retains the sod topology when performed in n-butanol,[6] in the absence of solvent the zni topology of ZIF-61 represents the lowest-energy structure for the \(Z_{\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{I m})}(\mathsf{m}\mathsf{I m})\) composition (Figure S22).
To understand the influence of the atmosphere on VPLE of CHO-Hlm in ZIF-8, trials were performed under argon, nitrogen, and air (Figure 4). After \(24~\mathrm{h}\), CHO-Im incorporation under argon and nitrogen reached \(30\ \%\) , compared to \(47\ \%\) under vacuum. Likely, the vacuum conditions enhance the vaporization rate and gas phase diffusion of the incoming linker through an increased mean free path length. Tests with ambient air in the reaction vessel led to VPLE rates similar as under vacuum. We hypothesized that moisture in the ambient air aids VPLE, compared to dry argon and nitrogen.[25] The catalyzing effect of moisture was confirmed by adding water vapor after evacuating the reaction vessel, to achieve \(5\ \%\) relative humidity at \(298~\mathrm{K}\). VPLE under these conditions led to the incorporation of \(63\ \%\) CHO-Im after \(24\,\mathfrak{h}\). Since neither ZIF-8 nor ZIF-90 have significant water uptakes at such a low relative humidity (Figure S23), the effect is likely due to co-adsorption of water with the incoming linker. We hypothesize that, similar as for acid gases in ZIFs, the incoming linker and water form a more reactive complex.[26]
The distribution of the CHO-Im linker in large \(150~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) ZIF-8 crystals after VPLE was investigated. Solid-state \(^1\mathsf{H}\) NMR spindiffusion measurements were performed for VPLE samples with a ZIF-849-90s1 composition (ZIFVPLE). These measurements were benchmarked against a mixed-linker ZIF material obtained
through direct solvothermal synthesis and with a nearly identical composition, ZIF-852-90s8 (ZIFDS). For mixed-linker ZIFs prepared by direct synthesis, the homogenous distribution of the mlm and CHO-Im linkers was previously confirmed through similar NMR experiments.[27] The signal highlighted in Figure 5a corresponds to the polarization transfer between the aldehyde and methyl group of CHO-Im and mlm, respectively. This result confirms the close proximity of both linkers in ZIFDS. In contrast, the absence of this signal in ZIFVPLE (Figure 5b) indicates the formation of segregated domains. This observation is in accordance with the spectrum of physical mixtures of ZIF-8 and ZIF-90 crystals, i.e. when both domains are completely segregated, and is further supported by the spin diffusion curves (Figures S24-25).[27]
To further understand the linker segregation in ZIFVPLE, the same crystals were imaged using stimulated Raman scattering (SRS). This technique allows to probe vibrational modes at a selected depth within the crystals. SRS microscopy confirms the
## COMMUNICATION
increasing intensity of the carbonyl band at \(1690~\mathsf{cm}^{-1}\) with increasing CHO-Im incorporation (Figure S26). Visualizing the CHO-Im distribution based on this vibration band reveals a higher concentration in the crystal core, throughout the VPLE process (Figure 5c-d). These results are in contrast to the linker distribution after solution-based linker exchange. In that case, NMR experiments also show non-perfect mixing, but with a higher CHO-Im concentration in the shell due to diffusion limitations.[16] Since VPLE is not diffusion-limited (vide supra), other factors must determine the linker distribution. The large ZIF-8 crystals are synthesized in the presence of sodium formate as a modulator. In the presence of sodium formate, ZIF-8 initially grows via a nanoparticle aggregation, before transitioning to a linker-particle attachment.[28] We hypothesize that this growth mode results in defects in the core of the large crystals, and that linker exchange initially occurs at these defect sites, as suggested by theoretical calculations for ZIFs[29,30] and experimentally observed in other MOFs.[31] The resulting local changes in pore polarity and hydrogen bonding capability likely favor the adsorption and incorporation of further CHO-HIm molecules. Such autocatalytic behaviour has been observed for Zn-N bond cleavage in ZIFs upon exposure to humid acid gases, and was explained by a simultaneously increasing water uptake and defectivity.[26] Additionally, the ability of incorporated CHO-Im linkers to act as both H-bond donors and acceptors might facilitate linker exchange at adjacent sites.[32] These effects are thought to be responsible for the observed CHO-Im domain formation in large ZIF-8 crystals after VPLE.In conclusion, VPLE is a novel method for solvent-free linker exchange in MOFs. The method was demonstrated for ZIF-8 with a range of imidazoles and the criteria governing the process were established. Since VPLE was demonstrated for thin films as well, we foresee its combination with the chemical vapor deposition of MOFs,[9] to form a vapor-only processing toolbox for the microfabrication of chemical sensors.[33]
In conclusion, VPLE is a novel method for solvent-free linker exchange in MOFs. The method was demonstrated for ZIF-8 with a range of imidazoles and the criteria governing the process were established. Since VPLE was demonstrated for thin films as well, we foresee its combination with the chemical vapor deposition of MOFs,[9] to form a vapor-only processing toolbox for the microfabrication of chemical sensors.[33]
